xnu 0156: the touch SPI delay uses the clock it compares against

The bit-bang asks for an 800 ns half period and gets 103 us: the tick
count came from ml_get_timebase(), which on this board is not the clock
mach_absolute_time() counts on. The measured cost was 80 s of wire time
for the 98 KB sections (the ack wait was 2.7 s). Convert with the
kernel's own nanoseconds_to_absolutetime() instead; the half period is
then the one asked for and the 220 KB firmware load should fall from
about three minutes to about ten seconds.
--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_touch.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_touch.c
@@ -652,7 +652,14 @@
 	if (ns == 0) {
 		return;
 	}
-	ticks = ((uint64_t)ns * mondrian_spi_hz) / 1000000000ull + 1;
+	/*
+	 * ml_get_timebase() is not the clock mach_absolute_time() counts on
+	 * this board: the old arithmetic asked for 2.5 us a bit and produced
+	 * 103 (the whole 220 KB firmware load took 160 s). nanoseconds_to_
+	 * absolutetime() is the kernel's own conversion for the clock the
+	 * loop compares against, so the half period is the one asked for.
+	 */
+	nanoseconds_to_absolutetime(ns, &ticks);
 	uint64_t start = mach_absolute_time();
 
 	while (mach_absolute_time() - start < ticks) {
